{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere","id":1074903,"title":"Having a hard time buying into the Tremere","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-04-07T16:37:07-07:00","id":1074903,"title":"Having a hard time buying into the Tremere","content":"First off, I'm NOT JUST talking about a group of mages actually turning into vampires.<br /> <br /> Althoough it seems unlikely that the Tremere would NOT test the magic elixir before drinking it and instead, would simply say &quot;FUCK IT, LETS JUST DRINK THE POTION AND SEE WHAT HAPPENS.&quot;<br /> <br /> Hermetic Mages are not exactly known for their utter sloppiness (except when they're downright pissed, which presumably wasn't the case at the moment that Tremere and his seven apprentices were about to slam back the elixir of unlife). Not testing the potions on some lackey (who would then turn into a vampire) seems unlikely at best.<br /> <br /> Now, I retconned the story to be more like &quot;The Diedne cast a death curse on the Tremere that was withering them away and they knew they were going to turn into vampires because they were on death's door and out of options and the only thing that would stave of their destruction was the static inducing power of Vitae.&quot; Still though, this lacks some satisfaction for me.<br /> <br /> I also have trouble believing that even though they could be outnumbered 100 to 1, the Hermetics, with Pillars or Spheres (and no Paradox according to Dark Ages Mage) woluld have trouble fighting off the Tremere or at least striking the key leaders of the clan in order to force them to order their followers to leave the Order alone.<br /> <br /> Does anyone else feel this way?<br /> <br /> Am I the only one who thinks Hermetic Mages wouldn't just jump into potential Vampirism.<br /> <br /> Is it really that likely that an order of awakened Mages, as limited in number as they are, would so easily fall prey to the Tremere and their Thaumaturgy?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3148,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3148-zennis","user":"Zennis","date":". 04-07-2017, 07:41 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3148-zennis","name":"Zennis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3148_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"414"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-07T16:38:09-07:00","id":1074905,"title":"","content":"Hubris is a hell of a drug","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13009-burritomage","name":"BurritoMage","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13009_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"585"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-07T17:34:16-07:00","id":1074919,"title":"","content":"I hear you, a bunch of angry Hermetics, high on pride and pissed that one of their own &quot;fell to the pathetic state of Vampirism&quot; might just say &quot;we will easily destroy them all and declare total war without further discussion!!!&quot; I could see that for sure.<br /> <br /> The whole Tremere drinking the potion without a second thought seems a little unbelievable though, hubris or no.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3148-zennis","name":"Zennis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3148_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"414"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-07T17:38:40-07:00","id":1074920,"title":"","content":"IIRC, it was heavily implied in the Transylvania Chronicles series from Vampire that Tremere may have been mentally pushed into his decision by Saulot as part of some sort of long game con to help him avoid having to deal with any other 3rd generation vampires or his own whiney clan until Gehenna, possibly as part of a plan to become the Demon Emperor or something. Because that's how Vampire rolled.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-07T17:43:51-07:00","id":1074921,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Zennis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere?p=1074919#post1074919\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The whole Tremere drinking the potion without a second thought seems a little unbelievable though, hubris or no.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think you are not taking in account the mind set of a man with a cult of personality behind desperate for &quot;true&quot; immortality. <br /> <br /> Also this is the dark ages, I always figured them quaffing a potion to become a vampire was pretty tame compared to the nonsense that went down at mist ridge before the order of reason turned the place into a parking lot.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13009-burritomage","name":"BurritoMage","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13009_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"585"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-07T17:55:39-07:00","id":1074924,"title":"","content":"Did they just drink potion\u200bs? I thought they made vampires turn them the normal way.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1503-faradn","name":"Faradn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1503_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"792"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-07T18:04:41-07:00","id":1074928,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Faradn</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere?p=1074924#post1074924\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Did they just drink potion\u200bs? I thought they made vampires turn them the normal way.</div> </div> </div> </div>No they drank some potion that Goratrix, Tremere's right hand man, cooked up. Apparently, being turned into vampires was NOT the desired effect and came as something of a surprise.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3148-zennis","name":"Zennis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3148_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"414"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-07T20:18:29-07:00","id":1074964,"title":"","content":"The stranger thing is that they survived doing so. These are creatures who have to sleep all day, and take incredible amounts of damage from plain old sunlight. This is a weakness so severe that mundane human hunters are a serious threat to them if they don't live unlives of perfect secrecy. They should have been slaughtered by their fellow mages after becoming these helpless abominations.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3598-cajunkhan","name":"CajunKhan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3598_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1700"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-07T20:42:54-07:00","id":1074971,"title":"","content":"That's a bit chicken before the egg, the history of Clan Tremere was written well before Mage was made.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13009-burritomage","name":"BurritoMage","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13009_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"585"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-07T20:57:40-07:00","id":1074974,"title":"","content":"It's beyond surprising that the Tremere were able to survive not only against a pissed off Order of Hermes, but also against several angry Voivodas. Antoher problem it's the idea that a bunch of Neonates were able to convert almost the entire House into vampires, presumably against their will, and with almost no one discovering it until it was too late<br /> <br /> I can think on some strategies, like hiding behind blood bound mages and using them to fool the OoH to believe that the problems of vampirism within the house were being caused by the attacks of the other vampires, in order to divert (or at least avert) fire until it was too late, but it still seems hard to swallow. It's just not possible to believe that a &quot;clan&quot; of Neonates would be able to pull off such a dangerous game.<br /> <br /> And about the whole drinking the potion of vampiric curse. I blame Tremere who either know the consequences and wanted to be vampire (as implied by Blood Treachery) or was manipulated by Saulot (or both). Tremere had total control of his apprentices, they where his most loyal even before the embrace, that's why they didn't questioned. Of course Tremere wouldn't fail them","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-07T21:56:54-07:00","id":1074992,"title":"","content":"The first thing to remember is that when the Tremere backstory was written Mage didn't exist yet, and &quot;Mages&quot; turned into something very different from what had initially been imagined between concept and execution. So yeah, everything is going to be a bit shoe-horned from the word go. That being said, there have been some cogent attempts to square that circle and some additional factors that bear consideration.<br /> <br /> Its not actually accurate to say there was no paradox at that time, it just took a different form (the ravaging?). House Tremere had already been sustaining itself with immortality potions, but their effectiveness was waning due to the encroaching effects of paradox and the Circle of Seven feared that this heralded the end of Magick entirely. They didn't end up being correct, but based on the evidence at the time it wasn't an unreasonable supposition and as a result they're all still alive centuries later to know they made a mistake.<br /> <br /> Why didn't they test the potion? I'd go with pure hubris. They'd already been using crazy immortality concoctions for a while and if all the calculations were correct then they had no reason to waste a precious resource on a guinea pig. Alternatively, its equally likely that Goratrix claimed to have tested it and the others believed him. Why would Goratrix do that? Any answer to that question only makes the game more interesting.<br /> <br /> How did the Tremere survive and multiply? Well embracing their House would actually have been pretty easy. Dominate is a bitch unless you're wearing active Mind defenses, and the flat fact is that most Mages aren't paranoid twinked out knife-in-teeth PCs. The blood bond is even nastier, especially if you don't know what vampiric vitae is or how it works. Vampires were an outside context problem for the Order of Hermes, and by the time they realized the gravity of that threat House Tremere was lost. They failed in the Massasa War for two main reasons. One, Thaumaturgy is linear but it doesn't invoke paradox. Two, in an apocalyptic situation the trump card all vampires have is the ability to reproduce at a faster rate than any other supernatural. For every score of Tremere the Hermetics killed another two score could be sired before the sun even rose. The victory condition for the OoH was complete destruction of the Tremere, whereas all the Tremere had to do to win was keep existing. As vampires all they have is time.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2507-caitiff-primogen","name":"Caitiff Primogen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2507_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1351"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-07T23:04:04-07:00","id":1075016,"title":"","content":"Right. So. Let's start from the beginning.<br /> <br /> The House Tremere (and the whole Order of Hermes) were originally part of <b>Ars Magica</b>, where a small faction of the House had become vampires. Notably, Tremere himself was never a vampire in <b>Ars Magica</b>, and by <i>5th Edition</i>, the vampires were wiped out.<br /> <br /> Now, <b>Vampire</b> goes into the whole history of Clan Tremere - how they were afraid True Magick was dying, their quest for immortality and new ways of spellcasting - but the most important part is that they didn't <i>think</i> they would become vampires. In their fear and their hubris, they believed they has distilled the essence of immortality from vampires, without all the nasty side-effects. It was this fear and hubris (and possibly some manipulation) that lead them to take risks.<br /> <br /> Then <b>Mage</b> takes the whole thing and says &quot;House Tremere was part of the Order of Hermes that stupidly turned themselves into vampires because mutha fuckas couldn't adapt.&quot;<br /> <br /> Cheers!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If you don't use an Oxford comma, I feel bad for you, son,<br /> 'Cuz I got ninety-nine problems, but clarity ain't one.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/408-nothing","name":"nothing","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar408_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"572"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-08T04:35:45-07:00","id":1075076,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Zennis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere#post1074903\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Althoough it seems unlikely that the Tremere would NOT test the magic elixir before drinking it and instead, would simply say &quot;FUCK IT, LETS JUST DRINK THE POTION AND SEE WHAT HAPPENS.&quot;<br /> <br /> Hermetic Mages are not exactly known for their utter sloppiness (except when they're downright pissed, which presumably wasn't the case at the moment that Tremere and his seven apprentices were about to slam back the elixir of unlife). Not testing the potions on some lackey (who would then turn into a vampire) seems unlikely at best.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Consider that the Potion(s) was supposed to lenghten their immortality. Being a True Magickal Potion, testing it could very well ruin the effort. Testing could also be rendered immpassible if the number seven or some symbolism of leading the House of Tremere was involved in making it (or said by Goratrix to be involved). Summing this, I think it could be said that the Council of Seven trusted Goratrix on his word, rather than that they were sloppy.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3254-muad-dib","name":"Muad'Dib","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3254_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2059"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-08T04:57:17-07:00","id":1075082,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Caitiff Primogen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere?p=1074992#post1074992\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The first thing to remember is that when the Tremere backstory was written Mage didn't exist yet, and &quot;Mages&quot; turned into something very different from what had initially been imagined between concept and execution..</div> </div> </div> </div>That's very interesting. How were Mages initially imagined?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-08T06:16:18-07:00","id":1075096,"title":"","content":"(Just jumping in...)<br /> <br /> It's similarly important to remember that while Vampire(tm) came out 3 years before Mage(tm), the Tremere were predicated on Ars Magica(tm), and while the conceptual application of Magic(k)(tm) is a bit different there, it is not so removed from what was established later on in Mage(tm), meta-conceptually (particularly as things played out in later editions/settings).<br /> <br /> It was, as indicated by other posters above, a combination of factors: Tremere, as a highly charismatic/persuasive cult leader (and such people can get even some of the most intelligent-but-perhaps-less-than-wise of people to do the most outlandish things), sought out an alternate form of immortality (as the sources of Vis required to fuel/maintain the Hermetic's existing rituals were becoming more difficult to find even during the Dark Ages) and swayed others to join him. Those around him believed in his abilities and felt assured about the soundness of his proposed potion (which, despite perceptions, *was* &quot;tested&quot; before hand via observational &quot;embraces&quot; of how vampires did x, y and z&quot;); of course, what they didn't realize was that it would result in them becoming undead... and losing their Magic(k)(tm)... and their god-selves... and having to feed on living blood so as to transubstantiate it into Vitae/Vis/whatever-the-hell-we're-supposed-to-call-it-now-Goddammit-Tremere-what-have-you-had-us-do(?!).... But the advantages soon became acceptable to them, after Tremere's further assurances. (And, of course, Saulot subtly influencing Tremere no doubt helped guide things in a certain direction.)<br /> <br /> It's actually pretty clear/credible when you step back and take a look at it from the multiple factors/facets involved. Hell, it's surprising that such hadn't occurred even sooner, contextually speaking...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I have been around here for waaaayyyy too fucking long...","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1433-just-john-forever","name":"Just John, Forever...","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1433_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-08T10:01:04-07:00","id":1075164,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Caitiff Primogen</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Its not actually accurate to say there was no paradox at that time, it just took a different form (the ravaging?). House Tremere had already been sustaining itself with immortality potions, but their effectiveness was waning due to the encroaching effects of paradox and the Circle of Seven feared that this heralded the end of Magick entirely. They didn't end up being correct, but based on the evidence at the time it wasn't an unreasonable supposition and as a result they're all still alive centuries later to know they made a mistake.</div> </div> </div> </div>This it's true, to an extent...Paradox in Dark Ages it's not just different, but also a non issue 90% of the time. You only get to suffer Paradox when you Botch, and it's believed that some long standing magical practices (like certain immortality potions) become more costly in Vis to mantain. That's why they started the investigation.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> How did the Tremere survive and multiply? Well embracing their House would actually have been pretty easy. Dominate is a bitch unless you're wearing active Mind defenses, and the flat fact is that most Mages aren't paranoid twinked out knife-in-teeth PCs. The blood bond is even nastier, especially if you don't know what vampiric vitae is or how it works. </div> </div> </div>While all of this it's possible, I can't believe it so easily, \u00bfwhy?.<br /> Thake this things into consideration:<br /> 1\u00b0 Paranoia i't very common among mages, especially powerful ones. We are talking about the Mages who admit in their Trad Book to be constantly breaking the rule that forbids scrying on another mage (rule that exists for a reason, namely justified paranoia). Heck, the OoH couldn't have been created if Bonisagus hadn't invented the &quot;Parma Magica&quot; to keep a shield when talking with other mages, that's how paranoid mages are. Mind shield wasn't invented for no reason, it's actually pretty &quot;standard&quot; procedure.<br /> 2\u00b0 The Tremere didn't lived all in Ceoris, hidden from the sight of other mages<br /> 3\u00b0 Any mage with anything that resembles Prime can detect the &quot;wrongness&quot; in a vampire, even by accident, merely by looking at the flow of magick, same can be happen with Life. These two resources are within OoH grasp, and they're common.<br /> 4\u00b0 The Tremere were having problems with vampires from the point they entered in the lands of the Tzimisce uninvited. It didn't escalated into all out war until they did the ritual by capturing two Mathusalems, but it wasn't unheard off. Vampirism wasn't unknown.<br /> 5\u00b0 The Tremere were all Neonates without access to a lot of vampiric power. Thaumaturgy wasn't invented. Presumably one of these young vampires could have been subdued easily if found<br /> <br /> While I can buy that the Tremere quickly gained the control over whatever minnor dissidence there could be in Ceoris, it's too much to believe that a group of Neonates with low level Dominate managed to:<br /> A. Never find a Mind shield blocking their attempts<br /> B. Never find a mage that would notice anything &quot;undead&quot; in their magic (which would, at least, amplify the paranoia)<br /> C. Either call to Ceoris or convert every Tremere in the place without anyone noticing that the mages are either disappearing or being changed (see A and B)<br /> D. When one of the above happened, they never faced a resistence that could have ended the charade.<br /> <br /> There's many things that could have gone wrong. It's not impossible for this scheme to work, but it's hard to beliebe that it worked so well.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> They failed in the Massasa War for two main reasons. One, Thaumaturgy is linear but it doesn't invoke paradox. </div> </div> </div>This it's wrong because, as I mentioned, in DA Paradox was a non issue most of the time. You only incur on it in a Botch, and botching Thaumaturgy it's bad too.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Two, in an apocalyptic situation the trump card all vampires have is the ability to reproduce at a faster rate than any other supernatural. For every score of Tremere the Hermetics killed another two score could be sired before the sun even rose. The victory condition for the OoH was complete destruction of the Tremere, whereas all the Tremere had to do to win was keep existing. As vampires all they have is time. </div> </div> </div>While ths it's more reasonable, there's some important difficulties too:<br /> <br /> 1\u00b0 Vampires may be easy to create, but Disciplines aren't easy to learn. Neonates that can barely control their blood aren't a chalenge for experienced mages that have been fighting vampires for a long time. (especially because Paradox was almost a non issue)<br /> <br /> 2\u00b0 The Tremere had vampiric enemies that should have been able to end a clan of young Neonates without Disciplines even after mages decided to stop loosing their time and numbers against them.<br /> <br /> 3\u00b0 How many towns in Transilvania can you &quot;mass embrace&quot; before some forces other than mages steps on your toes and trashes this little scheme of yours?<br /> <br /> 4\u00b0 This apocaliptic scenario didn't happened. The Tremere aren't a clan who got &quot;recycled&quot; after most of it's members being anihilated over and over. Ceoris never fell during the Massa War. Plenty of the most important Tremere elders were Tremere in life. Antediluvian magic needs to be called at some point to justify all of this.<br /> <br /> And think that, after this, the Tremere still had enough power to fight in the Omen War against three/four Clans for centuries.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 04-08-2017, 01:04 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-08T11:39:09-07:00","id":1075194,"title":"","content":"I think we underestimate hubris as a factor though. I mean, sure, most Tremere mages didn't live in Ceoris, so they wouldn't have been among the first turned. But that would just make them sitting ducks later.<br /> <br /> There are a lot of psychological factors to consider, but as paranoid as many Hermetic mages tend to be, they're still indoctrinated in a culture that is big on fostering an &quot;us versus them&quot; mentality. The Order of Hermes vs. other Magickal practitioners. The House vs. other Houses. They're not used to looking at themselves critically. And they're arrogant. They look at the fall of other Houses and think &quot;well, obviously, that can't happen HERE.&quot; (And we've seen how the Order tends to continually end up blindsided by corruption in their own ranks even to today. One would think the fall of House Tremere would mean that they were watching closely, and things like House Janissary wouldn't happen...and yet...)<br /> <br /> Given that we're talking about a Dark Ages scenario, without the kind of mass information available, we have something else to consider: how many Mages would have any idea what a Vampire actually is? And certainly, they're not going to have the advantage of long term observation to detect the tell tale signs.<br /> <br /> You meet an elder from another Chantry. He reads very oddly to your life detection magic. He's pale, cold, and strange. But would you think Vampire? Or would you think &quot;Haha, Octavius screwed up a magickal rite!&quot;<br /> <br /> I can absolutely buy the idea that the average Hermetic Mage is too arrogant to think that some of his own people would ever resort to something like vampirism, assuming they even know what vampirism is, when they have all of creation at their disposal. So, if they do come in contact with a Ceoris vampire, they might not realize what they're seeing until it's too late. The Tremere vampires have the advantage of surprise and can target any internal skeptics first. And if anyone outside of the House starts saying &quot;Hey, I think your elders are vampires,&quot; then, given the House politics, the likely response is going to be a kneejerk defensiveness/denial. That other House is just jealous of our power/trying to undermine us/making trouble so they can seize the advantage.<br /> <br /> Especially if we start thinking about the games that House Tremere played in the past against House Diedne and others. The Tremere would have been LESS likely to accept the idea of corruption in their own House if the idea comes from outside. (Heck, if I were Tremere, I'd be seriously tempted to try to use that.)<br /> <br /> It would have to be very gradual, but I think if Tremere was careful and patient, spreading Vampirism as a plague one Chantry at a time, especially if he can use other Houses as a distraction, it could work very well. At least until he has the numbers to do a full takeover by force. And once he has all of his own house, he might be able to work similar campaigns against the other Houses for some time before they figure out what's going on.<br /> <br /> And of course, while this is happening, Tremere and his cronies start figuring out the basics of Thaumaturgy. So that by the time it becomes an all-out war, they're by no means unarmed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2430,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","user":"kalinara","date":". 04-08-2017, 02:44 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","name":"kalinara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2430_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"582"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-08T13:49:26-07:00","id":1075231,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kalinara</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think we underestimate hubris as a factor though. I mean, sure, most Tremere mages didn't live in Ceoris, so they wouldn't have been among the first turned. But that would just make them sitting ducks later.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hubris it's not stupidity, Hubris it's to think you can suck the blood of an Antediluvian and get away with it. Hubris it's to summon demons and specters, because *know* you can control them. Summoning a demon without a circle of protection it's not Hubris, it's being careless. Leaving a seemingly corrupted mage unchecked it's not Hubris, it's just being careless.<br /> <br /> And mages should *never* be sitting ducks. Especially not Tremere, who were very active politicians and conspirators. If a mage of your house starts acting wierd, you want to know why. It's preposteros to think that not once did a Neonate Tremere failed into approaching one of it's own and bite her. It's preposterous to think that, with the Willpower that mages have + potentially a Mind ward + Countermagic, Domination 1-3 never failed. That no mage managed to fend it's beyond believable.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> There are a lot of psychological factors to consider, but as paranoid as many Hermetic mages tend to be, they're still indoctrinated in a culture that is big on fostering an &quot;us versus them&quot; mentality. The Order of Hermes vs. other Magickal practitioners. The House vs. other Houses. They're not used to looking at themselves critically. And they're arrogant. They look at the fall of other Houses and think &quot;well, obviously, that can't happen HERE.&quot; (And we've seen how the Order tends to continually end up blindsided by corruption in their own ranks even to today. One would think the fall of House Tremere would mean that they were watching closely, and things like House Janissary wouldn't happen...and yet...)<br /> <br /> <br /> Given that we're talking about a Dark Ages scenario, without the kind of mass information available, we have something else to consider: how many Mages would have any idea what a Vampire actually is? And certainly, they're not going to have the advantage of long term observation to detect the tell tale signs.<br /> <br /> You meet an elder from another Chantry. He reads very oddly to your life detection magic. He's pale, cold, and strange. But would you think Vampire? Or would you think &quot;Haha, Octavius screwed up a magickal rite!&quot; </div> </div> </div>This it's true, to some extend, but all of hermetic mages are rival, even within their own House. There's no blindly trusting a member of your own House. You don't leave the door of your sanctum open so every member of your House can enter. And while hospitality demands you let a Hermetic mage inside the chantry, this does not meant he will have full access, or that he won't be spied.<br /> <br /> And vampires are notoriously weird compared with other types of corruption. Vampires are terrible hiding themselves among mortals. Even withot magic they are notoriously pale, don't breath, never go outside by night, sweat blood, avoid torches and other fires. An accident or a strong insult could get you into Frenzy, and put end to your &quot;masquerade&quot; (frenzy ruining a facade has happened to me playing vampire more than once). This it's why the Camarilla frowns upon keeping contact with the mortal world.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I can absolutely buy the idea that the average Hermetic Mage is too arrogant to think that some of his own people would ever resort to something like vampirism, assuming they even know what vampirism is, when they have all of creation at their disposal. So, if they do come in contact with a Ceoris vampire, they might not realize what they're seeing until it's too late. The Tremere vampires have the advantage of surprise and can target any internal skeptics first. And if anyone outside of the House starts saying &quot;Hey, I think your elders are vampires,&quot; then, given the House politics, the likely response is going to be a kneejerk defensiveness/denial. That other House is just jealous of our power/trying to undermine us/making trouble so they can seize the advantage.<br /> <br /> Especially if we start thinking about the games that House Tremere played in the past against House Diedne and others. The Tremere would have been LESS likely to accept the idea of corruption in their own House if the idea comes from outside. (Heck, if I were Tremere, I'd be seriously tempted to try to use that.) </div> </div> </div>I can buy into this too. Surely, Tremere mages might have tried to deal with this problem &quot;in House&quot;, to avoid a Wizard March against the whole House, and this would have bought Tremere some time, but I don't buy Tremere mages being sitting ducks.<br /> <br /> However, since the most important members of the House can't go in public, they would have a hard time fending against the accusations on a tribunal. Accusations of vampirism couldn't go for a long time without the Tremere being discovered.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It would have to be very gradual, but I think if Tremere was careful and patient, spreading Vampirism as a plague one Chantry at a time, especially if he can use other Houses as a distraction, it could work very well. At least until he has the numbers to do a full takeover by force. </div> </div> </div>On the contrary, I believe that it would have to be very fast. The more you wait, the more chances something unexpected go wrong and foil your plans. Remember that spying, while forbidden, it's rather common. Remember that all hermetic mages are rival. Declaring a March of Mages against a corrupted mage it's very profitable, because you can keep whatever stuff the mage had in it's possesion. And the newly created Tremere don't have True Magic (nor Thaumaturgy) to protect against anything that any mage might attempt against them. Combine that with an almost nonexistant knowledge of Disciplines and you see how this plan could have gone wrong more easily than not.<br /> <br /> And that's not counting that they had to survive against the Tzimisce, who weren't embraced yesterday. <hr /><br /> A much better way this could have worked it's, rather that turning most Tremere into vampires, turing them into bonded <b>ghouls</b>. The DA: Vampire books seem to imply that, in the begining, chantries of mages had a better chance against the Tzimisce than the young vampire-mages. Keeping most Tremere bonded, but mage, would provide the necessary magical firepower to fend against Tzimisce and a way to fool mages more easily. Ghouls are harder to detect, they may feel &quot;wrong&quot; to Prime, but they're not so evidently death/corrupted as vampires + they don't need to feed on blood, and their Beast it's more under control. Ghouls are much better at infiltrating mortal society. And, since they're bonded, they could always be transformed into Vampires after Thaumaturgy was developed, after the war started.<br /> <br /> The brutal attacks of the Tzimisce could make an excuse to call young Tremere to protect their main chantry, but Tremere never returning (or returning vampiric) would attract unwanted atention. Still, mages shouldn't be easily fooled. I don't buy that all of the House was converted, it would be too difficult to make such a move. Probably a lot of Tremere merely folded into other Houses after the corruption was discovered.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 04-08-2017, 04:57 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-08T14:03:56-07:00","id":1075234,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere?p=1075164#post1075164\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">While all of this it's possible, I can't believe it so easily, \u00bfwhy?.<br /> Thake this things into consideration:<br /> 1\u00b0 Paranoia i't very common among mages, especially powerful ones. We are talking about the Mages who admit in their Trad Book to be constantly breaking the rule that forbids scrying on another mage (rule that exists for a reason, namely justified paranoia). Heck, the OoH couldn't have been created if Bonisagus hadn't invented the &quot;Parma Magica&quot; to keep a shield when talking with other mages, that's how paranoid mages are. Mind shield wasn't invented for no reason, it's actually pretty &quot;standard&quot; procedure.<br /> 2\u00b0 The Tremere didn't lived all in Ceoris, hidden from the sight of other mages<br /> 3\u00b0 Any mage with anything that resembles Prime can detect the &quot;wrongness&quot; in a vampire, even by accident, merely by looking at the flow of magick, same can be happen with Life. These two resources are within OoH grasp, and they're common.<br /> 4\u00b0 The Tremere were having problems with vampires from the point they entered in the lands of the Tzimisce uninvited. It didn't escalated into all out war until they did the ritual by capturing two Mathusalems, but it wasn't unheard off. Vampirism wasn't unknown.<br /> 5\u00b0 The Tremere were all Neonates without access to a lot of vampiric power. Thaumaturgy wasn't invented. Presumably one of these young vampires could have been subdued easily if found<br /> <br /> While I can buy that the Tremere quickly gained the control over whatever minnor dissidence there could be in Ceoris, it's too much to believe that a group of Neonates with low level Dominate managed to:<br /> A. Never find a Mind shield blocking their attempts<br /> B. Never find a mage that would notice anything &quot;undead&quot; in their magic (which would, at least, amplify the paranoia)<br /> C. Either call to Ceoris or convert every Tremere in the place without anyone noticing that the mages are either disappearing or being changed (see A and B)<br /> D. When one of the above happened, they never faced a resistence that could have ended the charade.<br /> <br /> There's many things that could have gone wrong. It's not impossible for this scheme to work, but it's hard to beliebe that it worked so well.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> While ths it's more reasonable, there's some important difficulties too:<br /> <br /> 1\u00b0 Vampires may be easy to create, but Disciplines aren't easy to learn. Neonates that can barely control their blood aren't a chalenge for experienced mages that have been fighting vampires for a long time. (especially because Paradox was almost a non issue)<br /> <br /> 2\u00b0 The Tremere had vampiric enemies that should have been able to end a clan of young Neonates without Disciplines even after mages decided to stop loosing their time and numbers against them.<br /> <br /> 3\u00b0 How many towns in Transilvania can you &quot;mass embrace&quot; before some forces other than mages steps on your toes and trashes this little scheme of yours?<br /> <br /> 4\u00b0 This apocaliptic scenario didn't happened. The Tremere aren't a clan who got &quot;recycled&quot; after most of it's members being anihilated over and over. Ceoris never fell during the Massa War. Plenty of the most important Tremere elders were Tremere in life. Antediluvian magic needs to be called at some point to justify all of this.<br /> <br /> And think that, after this, the Tremere still had enough power to fight in the Omen War against three/four Clans for centuries.</div> </div> </div> </div>If we were talking about the Tremere suborning the entire Order of Hermes I'd agree that it would strain credulity, but we're not. House Tremere wasn't that huge, and their <i>leadership</i> were the first ones to become vampires. They're a mystery cult, and in addition to everything I've already said its also equally likely that they convinced acolytes to lower their defenses just by framing it as a trial or test. The pyramid is like that. Even if someone was shielded against Dominate (the first two levels of which would be sufficient for most circumstances and which is easily accessible even to Neonates), it wouldn't shield them from the blood bond. We have every reason to believe that the OoH wasn't aware of the properties of vampiric vitae because the Tremere had to go out of their way to study vampirism in the wild. If there were already a body of knowledge detailing vampiric abilities with the Order they would have just accessed that. Obviously not every single Mage even within House Tremere could be duped. Those that discovered the treachery were either outnumbered and killed or escaped to warn the rest of the Order. Indeed one has to assume that's how it was discovered. <br /> <br /> In-clan Disciplines are actually pretty easy to develop. That's why clans have an affinity for them and why Caitiff develop Disciplines even in isolation. Its true that the leadership and other elders of the Tremere survived the war, but how many others did they throw the wolves? The scope of the Omen War is also often overstated. They weren't at war with four clans, they were at war with an alliance of vampires with members from four clans. The Tzimisce remained feudal and insular, reticent to emerge from their manses and risk precious resources even to aid other Tzimisce. Especially to aid other Tzimisce. There's a reason their childer eventually rose up against them. They're dicks. Many vampires of other clans aided the Tzimisce in hunting the Tremere, but their clans didn't pool together and throw every one of their members at them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2507-caitiff-primogen","name":"Caitiff Primogen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2507_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1351"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-08T14:38:12-07:00","id":1075251,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheShadowMan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere?p=1075082#post1075082\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That's very interesting. How were Mages initially imagined?</div> </div> </div> </div>Basically all using Paths of Thaumaturgy from Vampire. One is included as a character in the back of Hunters Hunted. There's also a werewolf, with it and some stuff in Milwaukee by Night giving you a glimpse of the early ideas for them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-08T15:03:09-07:00","id":1075259,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere?p=1075251#post1075251\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Basically all using Paths of Thaumaturgy from Vampire. One is included as a character in the back of Hunters Hunted. There's also a werewolf, with it and some stuff in Milwaukee by Night giving you a glimpse of the early ideas for them.</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually, entirely like Ars Magicka. After all, White Wolf owned the rights to it for a while there, before it was sold off, and they created Mage as a replacement. When I read through 1st edition Mummy, I was surprised to see an ad for Ars Magicka in the back. But that was how things were.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-08T15:10:04-07:00","id":1075263,"title":"","content":"In ars magica and DA:M for the Order of Hermes the youthening potion gets progressively harder and harder to use and eventually fails. So there's no true immortality for them. Tremere who was the youngest of the founders of the Order(and possibly only surviving one... depending on fairy shenanigans). So Tremere deciding he'd rather become a vampire than die makes sense in this context.<br /> <br /> The Tremere as a house were a major organizing front for the order of Hermes at the time, while there were other houses dedicated to organization many where barely internally organized and had little interest in fighting the war, with many of the strongest fighters of the Order actively oposing the war.<br /> <br /> Kolduns basically ran on the rule of two. They were as likely to attack eachother when they showed up as the Tremere. The Tzim in generally were barely more organized same with the gangrel. <br /> <br /> <br /> Saulot actively threw his clan under the bus, he threw them so hard as part of his elaborate scheme to win the Jyhad with... thaum or something its confusing.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-08T15:18:37-07:00","id":1075270,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere?p=1075259#post1075259\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Actually, entirely like Ars Magicka. After all, White Wolf owned the rights to it for a while there, before it was sold off, and they created Mage as a replacement. When I read through 1st edition Mummy, I was surprised to see an ad for Ars Magicka in the back. But that was how things were.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hmm. For some reason I was thinking that a lot of 1st ed Thaumaturgy mechanics was based on ArsM stuff, but I don't think I read any ArsM books until after they left WW, so I could be entirely mistaken on that. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-08T15:26:55-07:00","id":1075272,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lian</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere?p=1075263#post1075263\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Saulot actively threw his clan under the bus, he threw them so hard as part of his elaborate scheme to win the Jyhad with... thaum or something its confusing.</div> </div> </div> </div>\u200bYeah, it was kind of :<ul><li>1: Fake my Destruction</li> <li>2: ???</li> <li>3: Profit! *</li> </ul><br /> *<sub>Exact definition of &quot;Profit&quot; may vary according to game line, gematriaic value of the date, and which side hair is parted on this week. </sub> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-08T16:48:06-07:00","id":1075301,"title":"","content":"I think it's worth looking at the fact that it's the Tremere leadership, including Tremere himself, who ended up vampires first. Even granting the Tremere/Hermetic tendency to compete against one another, I think there's still likely to be a certain measure of hierarchy-based trust toward ones' elders. At least until you feel powerful enough to challenge them.<br /> <br /> If one of the Council members visits a smaller Chantry and invites the members to take part in a grand ritual that just happens to contain vampire vitae, then he can likely blood bond a lot of them very quickly. Once bound, then they can undergo the full corruption of the Chantry at their leisure.<br /> <br /> I'm not sure it would have taken that long to develop at least the start of Thaumaturgy, given how much of Hermetic practice includes ritualistic practice already. Obviously Awakened magic isn't an option, but if we operate under the assumption that Unawakened Sorcerers also make up a part of the Tradition, as per Sorcerer Revised, then they've got the set up for linear magic already. They just have to adapt it to their new, built in power source.<br /> <br /> And even really basic Thaumaturgy (especially anything pyrokinetic) would be rather effective against vampires who have never seen anything of the sort before and would not have much idea on how to combat it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2430,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","user":"kalinara","date":". 04-09-2017, 12:44 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","name":"kalinara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2430_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"582"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-09T00:27:41-07:00","id":1075395,"title":"","content":"Hypothetically speaking, how hard would it be for the leaders of house Tremere at the time to distribute a blood-bonding vitae potion mixed in with their old longevity stuff as a secret Tremere-only &quot;new and improved&quot; longevity potion, perhaps with the instructions, just drink 3 over the course of 1 day? How many members of house Tremere were there at the time anyway?<br /> <br /> Edit: Also, is there any material on why the Tremere are more susceptible to the blood bond?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12480,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","user":"Spacecat","date":". 04-09-2017, 03:41 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","name":"Spacecat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12480_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"597"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-09T02:04:04-07:00","id":1075406,"title":"","content":"one thing I find is consistently overlooked in these debates is the very hermetic paradigm. Hermetics aren't technomancers, especially in the middle ages. They are religious mages, calling unto angels, gods and elemental spirits with regularity even when not actually using Spirit. So, when the whole house falls or becomes corrupted into undead things that look like a blight unto the tellurian from the umbra... Spirits are everywhere in the medieval and they have close enough contact with the order that they have Niphilim merit.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4340-warpwind","name":"Warpwind","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4340_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"608"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-09T02:28:32-07:00","id":1075411,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Warpwind</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere?p=1075406#post1075406\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">one thing I find is consistently overlooked in these debates is the very hermetic paradigm. Hermetics aren't technomancers, especially in the middle ages. They are religious mages, calling unto angels, gods and elemental spirits with regularity even when not actually using Spirit. So, when the whole house falls or becomes corrupted into undead things that look like a blight unto the tellurian from the umbra... Spirits are everywhere in the medieval and they have close enough contact with the order that they have Niphilim merit.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't quite see where you are going with this. The house becomes corrupted and spirits are everywhere, therefore what exactly?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","name":"Spacecat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12480_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"597"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-09T03:38:48-07:00","id":1075427,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Spacecat</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere?p=1075411#post1075411\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I don't quite see where you are going with this. The house becomes corrupted and spirits are everywhere, therefore what exactly?</div> </div> </div> </div>therefore the other hermetics who truck with exactly the same spirits will catch a word. If a hermetic house that summoned angels/elementals extensively turns undead the same spirits would report to either other mages or to their own superiors, who have regular contacts with other mages for sure. works fine with garou. Not to mention the corruption and horror going on in Ceoris, completely revolting.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":4340,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4340-warpwind","user":"Warpwind","date":". 04-09-2017, 06:54 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4340-warpwind","name":"Warpwind","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4340_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"608"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-09T10:04:11-07:00","id":1075511,"title":"","content":"Hermetic paradigm isn't JUST angels and elementals. There is a lot of alchemy, geometry, and other stuff too. But that said, you're making a few assumptions here.<br /> <br /> 1. That the spirits consulted by the Hermetic mages are universal. If we operate under the idea that these angels are Astral creations, then there's nothing to say that Stephan of the Tremere and Marcus of Flambeau are talking to the same being when they call on the Archangel Raphael.<br /> <br /> 2. That Umbrood/Spirits/Angels have a comparable morality to the Hermetic mages themselves, and would see fit to report to other Hermetic mages. They may simply not care what the mortals that communicate with them get up to. Certainly, we've seen plenty of examples of corrupt and evil Hermetics, who aren't Vampires. And Hermetics aren't the only ones who call on Angels: Ksirafai, Choristers, and some Barabbi do as well. I haven't read every sourcebook, but I don't remember seeing references to tattling spirits.<br /> <br /> And even if they have the same morality, they might well see this as a test. God did give humans free will after all.<br /> <br /> 3. That every use of Angels/Spirits/Umbrood as a focus actually calls down their attention/opens communication. If we are talking about a universal set of religious or astral figures, then they would likely have a LOT of people calling their attention.<br /> <br /> 4. That, even IF the Angels/Spirits/Umbrood warned a couple of people, those people would actually be listened to.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","name":"kalinara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2430_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"582"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-09T11:49:59-07:00","id":1075533,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Spacecat</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hypothetically speaking, how hard would it be for the leaders of house Tremere at the time to distribute a blood-bonding vitae potion mixed in with their old longevity stuff as a secret Tremere-only &quot;new and improved&quot; longevity potion, perhaps with the instructions, just drink 3 over the course of 1 day? How many members of house Tremere were there at the time anyway?</div> </div> </div> </div>Distributing the potion without it being stolen or lost in a world where &quot;mail&quot; is a guy with a horse riding through europe. Magic(k) can sidestep this issue, to some extent.<br /> <br /> Mages, being curious and paranoid, would surely want to scan the potion, and may get suspicious when they see the component of evil death magic resonance in it, but this doesn't mean that no one it's going to drink it anyway. Ironically, if they identify the component as vampiric blood, they may trust the potion more than if they don't know what the fuck it is (assuming they know about vampires, but not the properties of vitae).<br /> <br /> The real problem it's that mages don't share their potions of longevity. A gift like that it's very rare. Yous superiors suddenly deciding to bring you something like that would be very suspicious.<br /> <br /> Abusing of the rank to make young mages go to Transilvania (to defend the House against vampiric attacks?) and then partake in a ritual with vampiric blood (we're totally using Simpathetic magic to create a ward to repel vampires with this blood) seems more plausible.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Edit: Also, is there any material on why the Tremere are more susceptible to the blood bond? </div> </div> </div>Wasn't because every Tremere drinks a potion with the Vitae of the Council of Seven?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-09T13:16:12-07:00","id":1075564,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere?p=1075533#post1075533\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Abusing of the rank to make young mages go to Transilvania (to defend the House against vampiric attacks?) and then partake in a ritual with vampiric blood (we're totally using Simpathetic magic to create a ward to repel vampires with this blood) seems more plausible.</div> </div> </div> </div>I suppose that would be a more certain solution, with little chance of being derailed by unforeseen circumstances.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere?p=1075533#post1075533\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Wasn't because every Tremere drinks a potion with the Vitae of the Council of Seven?</div> </div> </div> </div>It would be nice if someone could confirm these are cause and effect or confirm that the material is truly ambiguous on this matter. The wording in M20 makes it sound like the weakness is the cause of this practice rather than the result.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","name":"Spacecat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12480_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"597"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-09T16:07:00-07:00","id":1075616,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Spacecat</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere?p=1075395#post1075395\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hypothetically speaking, how hard would it be for the leaders of house Tremere at the time to distribute a blood-bonding vitae potion mixed in with their old longevity stuff as a secret Tremere-only &quot;new and improved&quot; longevity potion, perhaps with the instructions, just drink 3 over the course of 1 day? How many members of house Tremere were there at the time anyway?<br /> <br /> Edit: Also, is there any material on why the Tremere are more susceptible to the blood bond?</div> </div> </div> </div>As point of order it would have to be three drinks over three nights, not three in one night. No matter how much vitae you drink if it's all in one night you're only one step blood bound. Three drinks over three nights is just how the magic works. <br /> <br /> The Tremere weakness in V20 is an alteration of their weakness in earlier editions where they were always considered to be one step blood bound to the Circle of Seven due to drinking from a cup of transubstantiated vitae either during or shortly after their embrace by rigidly observed clan protocol (in order to dodge it you had to buy a special clan merit). In V20 the weakness is thematically similar. The Tremere, much like the Assamites, have a lot of associated consumption imagery (even more than standard issue for vampires). The drinking of the potions that turned them into vampires, the devouring of the Salubri, the ritual of the Cup of the Seven which both cements loyalty and deliberately mirrors the origins of the Tremere and makes them &quot;true&quot; members rather than &quot;just&quot; vampires. As such their weakness reflects the perils of drinking too heavily.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2507-caitiff-primogen","name":"Caitiff Primogen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2507_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1351"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-09T16:51:27-07:00","id":1075630,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Caitiff Primogen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere?p=1075616#post1075616\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As point of order it would have to be three drinks over three nights, not three in one night. No matter how much vitae you drink if it's all in one night you're only one step blood bound. Three drinks over three nights is just how the magic works.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thanks for that clarification.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Caitiff Primogen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere?p=1075616#post1075616\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Tremere weakness in V20 is an alteration of their weakness in earlier editions where they were always considered to be one step blood bound to the Circle of Seven due to drinking from a cup of transubstantiated vitae either during or shortly after their embrace by rigidly observed clan protocol (in order to dodge it you had to buy a special clan merit). In V20 the weakness is thematically similar. The Tremere, much like the Assamites, have a lot of associated consumption imagery (even more than standard issue for vampires). The drinking of the potions that turned them into vampires, the devouring of the Salubri, the ritual of the Cup of the Seven which both cements loyalty and deliberately mirrors the origins of the Tremere and makes them &quot;true&quot; members rather than &quot;just&quot; vampires. As such their weakness reflects the perils of drinking too heavily.</div> </div> </div> </div>So they are more susceptible because of a combination of diablerie, potion drinking, and the overuse of blood bonding?<br /> <br /> Edit:<br /> <br /> Also, this is around the time there were less than a hundred mages per house right?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12480,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","user":"Spacecat","date":". 04-09-2017, 10:03 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","name":"Spacecat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12480_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"597"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-09T20:02:22-07:00","id":1075690,"title":"","content":"I have not read the entire thread but I keep seeing something in the first two pages that just throws me off: the idea that the early Tremere were &quot;just a group of neonates&quot;. That is just preposterous. The Mages involved were masters who were hundreds of years old BEFORE becoming Vampires. These people were not typical neonates. They were incredibly powerful, resourceful, and intelligent. The only thing they lost access to after becoming a vampire was their True Magick, and they later invented Thaumaturgy to overcome that! Dominate and Auspex are in clan as well, so they likely had those abilities to fall back on in the early days. If you can't imagine a scenario where a group of powerful Mages suddenly become Vampires in their quest for immortality and begin covertly converting others to their cause then I think you simply don't have enough imagination.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12710-pazuzuaxelf","name":"PazuzuAxelf","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-10T02:20:10-07:00","id":1075805,"title":"","content":"As mentioned above one of the big things to remember is that the early Tremere, especially Tremere, Goratrix, and Etrius were all Mages for over a century before they 'embraced' themselves. Once they have firsthand experience with vampire blood and its magical properties became clear they quickly began adapting their former knowledge to create the Thaumaturgical Paths they became famous for. Currently Black Hand Guide to Tal'Mahe'Ra has the most recent rules for what happens when a mage is embraced AFAIK.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Yet their occult knowledge remains. Former sorcerers learn magical Disciplines faster than other Kindred. To reflect this in a system, calculate the total Experience value of the magus\u2019 lost powers. She gains a rebate of half of this total to spend on Necromancy, Thaumaturgy, and other forms of blood magic. The vampire usually requires a teacher for anything learned out-of-Clan, but the Storyteller may make an exception if the Cainite used to practice a form of magic that strongly resembles an existing form of blood magic, or if the player and Storyteller develop a new Path to represent the fledgling\u2019s unnatural insights. This may explain how sorcerers such as the Tremere rapidly developed sorcery powerful enough to disrupt the status quo. </div> </div> </div>So a Mage with multiple Pillars/Spheres between 3-5 dots is going to have a far more significant repertoire of mystical oomph than a normal neonate could ever dream of in the beginning. Add to this the fact that much of the House structure beneath them still had all the awakened tricks at their disposal it becomes easier to see how they survived. By the time the Order moved against them the Tremere would already have prepared knowing their enemy well since they were them. Additionally Tremere had just orchestrated the destruction of House Diedne only a few decades ago through sheer personal persuasion. The Order may quickly start looking grimly at the losses of trying to go after and destroy another House prepared to fight them. Add in the fact that magic was becoming less predictable and the Order pulling back and eventually ignoring the Tremere so long as the latter did the same also makes sense. <br /> <br /> On the other side of the war bringing the awakened structure to fight the Omen War will pit the vampires against a foe whose abilities they do not understand. Once the Tremere convince the Ventrue to back them they can successfully stabilize since now they have a Tzimisce free region to move into and a potent ally to convince the other clans to just let the Tremere be.<br /> <br /> On the point of magic becoming less reliable. Tremere, Goratrix, and Etrius had all surpassed a normal mortal lifespan thus magic was the ONLY reason they were alive. They find out magic is starting to fail (they dont have a rulebook to see its only getting more difficult instead they see effects that once worked now DONT) and they panic. How long until the potions and methods they use to extend their life fail. If they do fail will they age normally or will they simply die instantly as their true age catches up. That terror of thinking you could just up and die without notice drove Tremere to put both Etrius and Goratrix on their mission to find another out to death. When Goratrix came saying that he had found an answer that would ensure their life in perpetuity of course they jumped at it. Why take the risk of spending time studying the potion when you might DIE at any moment, especially since its coming from the person you specifically told to go find a solution and is now offering it?<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2008-cynic01","name":"Cynic01","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"437"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-10T02:22:26-07:00","id":1075806,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PazuzuAxelf</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere?p=1075690#post1075690\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I have not read the entire thread but I keep seeing something in the first two pages that just throws me off: the idea that the early Tremere were &quot;just a group of neonates&quot;. That is just preposterous. The Mages involved were masters who were hundreds of years old BEFORE becoming Vampires. These people were not typical neonates. They were incredibly powerful, resourceful, and intelligent. The only thing they lost access to after becoming a vampire was their True Magick, and they later invented Thaumaturgy to overcome that! Dominate and Auspex are in clan as well, so they likely had those abilities to fall back on in the early days. If you can't imagine a scenario where a group of powerful Mages suddenly become Vampires in their quest for immortality and begin covertly converting others to their cause then I think you simply don't have enough imagination.</div> </div> </div> </div>Indeed. For example the problem with spirits telling others about Tremere becoming Kindred...this could have been relatively easily solved by the Seven, or other Tremere, misdirecting or commanding the spirits away. The Council's of Seven knowledge about spirits wouldn't become a non-factor post-Embrace, I'd say.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3254-muad-dib","name":"Muad'Dib","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3254_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2059"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-10T05:45:58-07:00","id":1075846,"title":"","content":"I take it as a given that it happened. Doesn't seem too odd to me, a group of seven or so vampire wizards corrupting an organization of maybe a hundred wizards that they are already more or less in control of. I'm more interested in how exactly it happened.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","name":"Spacecat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12480_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"597"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-10T05:54:08-07:00","id":1075847,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Spacecat</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere?p=1075846#post1075846\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I take it as a given that it happened. Doesn't seem too odd to me, a group of seven or so vampire wizards corrupting an organization of maybe a hundred wizards that they are already more or less in control of. I'm more interested in how exactly it happened.</div> </div> </div> </div>The <i>House of Tremere</i> book adds involvement of two powerful spirits - Root of All and Stars Above.<br /> <br /> I also see it being written between the lines that Tremere was influenced and perhaps aided in choosing Saulot as the one to seek out for Diablerie. His original choice was the Antediluvian of the Followers of Set, if I recall correctly.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3254-muad-dib","name":"Muad'Dib","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3254_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2059"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-10T06:39:12-07:00","id":1075860,"title":"","content":"So far, I'm seeing it as:<br /> <br /> Magick gets wonky and the elders of Tremere panic<br /> Goratrix gives the elders a formula that turns them into vampires<br /> They try to make the best of a bad situation by converting house Tremere from a Wizard House with Vampires to The Vampire Wizard House<br /> They decide to eat Saulot<br /> They gain allies and become &quot;Legit&quot;<br /> <br /> I'm still working on where in this list do the spirits come in and also the wars with the vampires, when the Order finds out, and where the Gargoyles figure in all this.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","name":"Spacecat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12480_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"597"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-10T07:31:22-07:00","id":1075874,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PazuzuAxelf</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">the idea that the early Tremere were &quot;just a group of neonates&quot;. That is just preposterous. The Mages involved were masters who were hundreds of years old BEFORE becoming Vampires. These people were not typical neonates. They were incredibly powerful, resourceful, and intelligent. The only thing they lost access to after becoming a vampire was their True Magick</div> </div> </div> </div><i>&quot;Only lost acces to their True Magick&quot;.</i> Without <i>that</i> an archmaster it's but a mortal with a relatively good amount of xp (dependant on how much was to the magick) already spent, and good backgrounds. <br /> You cannot just throw a statement like that, you need to be able to explain how this would be used to easily subvert a house of hermes and hide a vampiric conspiracy from mages (of all splats) for enough time to develop Thaumaturgy. And make it sound like more than a mere coincidence.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> and they later invented Thaumaturgy to overcome that! </div> </div> </div>The key word here it's <b>later</b>.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Dominate and Auspex are in clan as well, so they likely had those abilities to fall back on in the early days. </div> </div> </div>You have Dominate and Auspex at Neonate levels, I grant you that. How are you going to use these to your advantage without calling more attention than you would otherwise?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cynic01</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Currently Black Hand Guide to Tal'Mahe'Ra has the most recent rules for what happens when a mage is embraced AFAIK.</div> </div> </div> </div>This rule it's non existant in the setting. It's purpose it's to give a PC something for the xp they wasted in their previous splat, but it doesn't represent how the Embrace really ever worked in the setting. The Embrace kills your magick, period. Thaumaturgy wasn't developped in a few days. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Muad'Dib</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Indeed. For example the problem with spirits telling others about Tremere becoming Kindred...this could have been relatively easily solved by the Seven, or other Tremere, misdirecting or commanding the spirits away. The Council's of Seven knowledge about spirits wouldn't become a non-factor post-Embrace, I'd say.</div> </div> </div> </div>And how exactly would you beneffit from this knowledge without magic to summon or command them? <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-10T07:39:30-07:00","id":1075877,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Spacecat</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm still working on where in this list do the spirits come in and also the wars with the vampires, when the Order finds out, and where the Gargoyles figure in all this.</div> </div> </div> </div>While I don't remember the answers to most of this important questions, I can tell you that Gargoyles were created as a weapon in the Omen War, which wasn't the unimportant skirmish that some here seem to believe. Ceoris went under siege quite a number of times. I remember that Gargoyles were used to defend this very important fortress.<br /> <br /> There's an old thread where this things were discussed in depth. Including stuff as when did the Order start and end the Wizard March, and when the Omen War began.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-10T08:28:27-07:00","id":1075904,"title":"","content":"With regards to the how of things, I'm of the opinion that not all the original conspirators that led to the conversion of the house became vampires at the same time. The clanbook mentions Etrius objected to the idea of undeath &amp; the Lore of the Clans wording on the matter says &quot;Several emerged from it not just as immortals, but also as vampires&quot; and &quot;Those who were already vampires set about converting the rest of the order.&quot; not, &quot;They all became vampires&quot; or &quot;Then they all set about to converting the rest of the order&quot;. With a few conspirator mages not yet converted and maybe hanging around waiting for the vampires to develop &quot;vampire magic&quot; before joining their number, probably some knowledge of blood bonding, and the fact that the highest ranking members of the order are involved, I can see how this could easily cascade into converting around a hundred people fairly quickly and relatively quietly. A group with this sort of setup could easily rely on Authority Pressure and Organizational Culture to get much of the work done.<br /> <br /> Edit: Also,<br /> &quot;Yet their occult knowledge remains. Former sorcerers learn magical Disciplines faster than other Kindred. To reflect this in a system, calculate the total Experience value of the magus\u2019 lost powers. She gains a rebate of half of this total to spend on Necromancy, Thaumaturgy, and other forms of blood magic. The vampire usually requires a teacher for anything learned out-of-Clan, but the Storyteller may make an exception if the Cainite used to practice a form of magic that strongly resembles an existing form of blood magic, or if the player and Storyteller develop a new Path to represent the fledgling\u2019s unnatural insights. This may explain how sorcerers such as the Tremere rapidly developed sorcery powerful enough to disrupt the status quo.&quot;<br /> <br /> Doesn't this pretty much mean that a Tremere master mage with 5 dots in 2 spheres and arete 5 when turned into a vampire could intuitively and very quickly have Thaumaturgy 5, two secondary paths of Thaumaturgy at 3 dots and 1 dot in some other discipline?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12480,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","user":"Spacecat","date":". 04-10-2017, 05:40 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","name":"Spacecat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12480_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"597"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-10T21:42:41-07:00","id":1076287,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Spacecat</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere?p=1075904#post1075904\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">With regards to the how of things, I'm of the opinion that not all the original conspirators that led to the conversion of the house became vampires at the same time. The clanbook mentions Etrius objected to the idea of undeath &amp; the Lore of the Clans wording on the matter says &amp;quot;Several emerged from it not just as immortals, but also as vampires&amp;quot; and &amp;quot;Those who were already vampires set about converting the rest of the order.&amp;quot; not, &amp;quot;They all became vampires&amp;quot; or &amp;quot;Then they all set about to converting the rest of the order&amp;quot;. With a few conspirator mages not yet converted and maybe hanging around waiting for the vampires to develop &amp;quot;vampire magic&amp;quot; before joining their number, probably some knowledge of blood bonding, and the fact that the highest ranking members of the order are involved, I can see how this could easily cascade into converting around a hundred people fairly quickly and relatively quietly. A group with this sort of setup could easily rely on Authority Pressure and Organizational Culture to get much of the work done.<br /> <br /> Edit: Also,<br /> &amp;quot;Yet their occult knowledge remains. Former sorcerers learn magical Disciplines faster than other Kindred. To reflect this in a system, calculate the total Experience value of the magus\u2019 lost powers. She gains a rebate of half of this total to spend on Necromancy, Thaumaturgy, and other forms of blood magic. The vampire usually requires a teacher for anything learned out-of-Clan, but the Storyteller may make an exception if the Cainite used to practice a form of magic that strongly resembles an existing form of blood magic, or if the player and Storyteller develop a new Path to represent the fledgling\u2019s unnatural insights. This may explain how sorcerers such as the Tremere rapidly developed sorcery powerful enough to disrupt the status quo.&amp;quot;<br /> <br /> Doesn't this pretty much mean that a Tremere master mage with 5 dots in 2 spheres and arete 5 when turned into a vampire could intuitively and very quickly have Thaumaturgy 5, two secondary paths of Thaumaturgy at 3 dots and 1 dot in some other discipline?</div> </div> </div> </div>No, it doesn't. What Aleph said above is actually correct, you do NOT simply get free thaumaturgy right away. It stills takes time to develop.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3148-zennis","name":"Zennis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3148_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"414"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-10T22:03:04-07:00","id":1076301,"title":"","content":"How much time? Can you quantify that? By this system, the XP to spend is immediately available. What would training times be like? 1 dot per story?<br /> Why I ask is because I'd like to understand what sort of creatures these guys would be in the early stages of the development of the Tremere as a Vampire Clan.<br /> <br /> If we use the one dot per trait per story limitation (whatever 1 story means), you would want to gain several (as many as they can with what methods they could devise) disciplines at 1 as soon as possible, and then raise your Thaumaturgy to 2 by your second &quot;story&quot; so that you could purchase numerous paths at 1.<br /> <br /> Edit: By mage reckoning, 1 story is something more than one day and less than 6 months. Does Vampire have a statement regarding the length of a story?<br /> <br /> Edit 2: With regards to timeline, in 1022 Tremere kills and experiments on a Tzimisce and starts the whole conversion thing, it's only in 1199 that a Tribunal uncovers evidence of house Tremere vampirism (enough to bring to Doissetep) and Doissetep only acknowledges it in 1201.<br /> <br /> Edit 3: Seems that the early Tremere Vampire Warlocks would have a spread of Disciplines and paths rated from 2 to 3 within a year.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12480,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","user":"Spacecat","date":". 04-11-2017, 02:05 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","name":"Spacecat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12480_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"597"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-11T03:26:08-07:00","id":1076395,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kalinara</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere?p=1075511#post1075511\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hermetic paradigm isn't JUST angels and elementals. There is a lot of alchemy, geometry, and other stuff too. But that said, you're making a few assumptions here.<br /> <br /> 1. That the spirits consulted by the Hermetic mages are universal. If we operate under the idea that these angels are Astral creations, then there's nothing to say that Stephan of the Tremere and Marcus of Flambeau are talking to the same being when they call on the Archangel Raphael.<br /> <br /> 2. That Umbrood/Spirits/Angels have a comparable morality to the Hermetic mages themselves, and would see fit to report to other Hermetic mages. They may simply not care what the mortals that communicate with them get up to. Certainly, we've seen plenty of examples of corrupt and evil Hermetics, who aren't Vampires. And Hermetics aren't the only ones who call on Angels: Ksirafai, Choristers, and some Barabbi do as well. I haven't read every sourcebook, but I don't remember seeing references to tattling spirits.<br /> <br /> And even if they have the same morality, they might well see this as a test. God did give humans free will after all.<br /> <br /> 3. That every use of Angels/Spirits/Umbrood as a focus actually calls down their attention/opens communication. If we are talking about a universal set of religious or astral figures, then they would likely have a LOT of people calling their attention.<br /> <br /> 4. That, even IF the Angels/Spirits/Umbrood warned a couple of people, those people would actually be listened to.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> 1. yes. pretty sure it was noted in... book of worlds? and besides reading around about spirits its pretty clear all hermetics contact the same beings. now, choristers probably don't call on the same angels as hermetics, but it is said in Blood Treachery that every hermetic angel is an unicque individual.<br /> 2. extensively noted that spirits don't like vampires, a lot. nobody likes vampires, even other vampires.<br /> 3. and only a tiny fraction of them are mages. and YES they are important. <br /> 4. that's the point. they would contact a LOT of people. because those beings are universal for hermetic paradigm and what's more they have high reputation and are verifiable.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4340-warpwind","name":"Warpwind","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4340_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"608"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-11T06:46:06-07:00","id":1076449,"title":"","content":"With regards to Hermeticism and Spirits; from my shallow understanding of Hermeticism, I've seen little emphasis on the average practitioner consulting the spirits and so much more emphasis on the evocation of these for very specific purposes and the self-identification with spirits with the intent of gaining henosis (union) with these. Actual conversations with the spirits seems to have been somewhat limited to certain people. I'm also supposing that the perpetrators of this whole vampire conversion thing would have stopped using the Hermetic practice of theurgy and thus would have no further contact with the vast majority of the sorts of spirits that the other hermetics would be contacting. Theurgy doesn't seem very compatible with taking immortality from vampires anyway. They'd probably fall back on goetia with a heavy reliance on specific spirits they would assume no one else would be contacting much and (since this is the WoD) on Magia, magic as an extension of the will.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","name":"Spacecat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12480_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"597"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-11T07:23:38-07:00","id":1076471,"title":"","content":"Spirits also generally aren't known for straightforward communication. Assuming that these spirits are the same, care enough about what specific mages are doing, have a specific opinion about vampires (honestly, I've never seen anything to indicate vampires have much if any interaction with the spirit world at all post-Embrace, and an aversion to them as a concept doesn't necessarily mean that the spirits care enough to actively report on them), and assuming the Tremere retained any contact with said spirits after their turn...<br /> <br /> Who's to say that those few handful of mages will actually understand what the spirits are trying to tell them? Or believe that the spirits aren't trying to trick them. (Many Hermetics follow King Solomon's example and summon Djinn. Djinn are not known for being trustworthy sources.)<br /> <br /> Also, Hermetics also aren't Dreamspeakers or Choristers. They may consult spirits, but it's generally less about seeking guidance and more about commanding them. There's a reason their paradigm/approach tends to include things like sacred geometry, Enochian language and so on. You're not going to see the same veneration for someone who communes with these beings as you would for other Traditions. Spirits are useful, but that's all.<br /> <br /> I have to admit, I'm still of the opinion that it shouldn't take that long to develop some kind of Thaumaturgy. I don't think we'd see five dot path skills right away, but the Tremere are already used to ritualized magic forms, linear magic techniques and the use of Tass. Depending on how much went into their study of their vampire subjects before the ritual, they may even be aware of practices like Koldunic sorcery, which would give them even more of an idea of where to direct their efforts. <br /> <br /> As mentioned above, it took more than a century for the other Hermetics to realize what's going on. And on the vampire side, the Tremere targets were primarily Tzimisce and Gangrel. The former tend toward isolation and privacy, while the latter tend to be nomadic, so in both cases, it's very possible that individual members could disappear for a while before others in the clans notice and trace their movements enough to detect a pattern. That should buy quite a bit of time to shore up their strength.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","name":"kalinara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2430_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"582"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-11T07:40:47-07:00","id":1076481,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kalinara</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere?p=1076471#post1076471\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I have to admit, I'm still of the opinion that it shouldn't take that long to develop some kind of Thaumaturgy.</div> </div> </div> </div>By my reckoning, at a maximum of 1 dot per trait per story, with each dot of discipline or path being a separate trait, and each story being less than 6 months, they'd be able to get their disciplines up to level 5 well within 2 years.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","name":"Spacecat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12480_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"597"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-11T08:43:09-07:00","id":1076519,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Spacecat</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">By my reckoning, at a maximum of 1 dot per trait per story, with each dot of discipline or path being a separate trait, and each story being less than 6 months, they'd be able to get their disciplines up to level 5 well within 2 years.</div> </div> </div> </div>\u200bYou have to think that, normaly, a starting character it's supposed to have some experience as vampire (usually with their sires). 10 to 50 years it's normal for starting player characters. You don't start as a complete newbie in Vampire.<br /> Also, keep in mind that the rules usually assume that you have soemone that teaches you Thaum, especially in the Black Hand. Tremere hadn't a master<br /> (he was the Master <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" />).<br /> <br /> I must add that Story it's useless as a unit of time. It's time it's infinitely variable because there isn't a strict measure for the &quot;time between stories&quot; (which it's the time you really use to develop skills).<br /> <br /> <br /> However I recognize that I should search how much time took Thaumaturgy to develop, because I don't know it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 04-11-2017, 11:46 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-11T08:53:52-07:00","id":1076521,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kalinara</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">As mentioned above, it took more than a century for the other Hermetics to realize what's going on.</div> </div> </div> </div>And you have no problem with the Hermetic being fooled <i>that </i>amount of time?. Because, to me, it seems less believable for a conspiration to be hidden for hundreds of years than for a few","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-11T09:46:21-07:00","id":1076549,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere?p=1076519#post1076519\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> \u200bYou have to think that, normaly, a starting character it's supposed to have some experience as vampire (usually with their sires). 10 to 50 years it's normal for starting player characters. You don't start as a complete newbie in Vampire.</div> </div> </div> </div>What does this have to do with learning to use new magical capabilities based on already developed magical knowledge granted to you by your experience as a mage? I don't follow.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere?p=1076519#post1076519\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also, keep in mind that the rules usually assume that you have soemone that teaches you Thaum, especially in the Black Hand. Tremere hadn't a master<br /> (he was the Master <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" />).</div> </div> </div> </div>So we're ignoring the add on rule of mages gaining XP when turning into vampires and the tutor exception of developing a paths that fit magical insights specifically meant to explain the Tremere?<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere?p=1076519#post1076519\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I must add that Story it's useless as a unit of time. It's time it's infinitely variable because there isn't a strict measure for the &quot;time between stories&quot; (which it's the time you really use to develop skills).</div> </div> </div> </div>Well in mage, they placed it between a day and 6 months, giving it an upper and lower limit.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere?p=1076519#post1076519\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">However I recognize that I should search how much time took Thaumaturgy to develop, because I don't know it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Please inform us if you do find that. I've been looking.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","name":"Spacecat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12480_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"597"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-11T10:05:35-07:00","id":1076561,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere?p=1076521#post1076521\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> And you have no problem with the Hermetic being fooled <i>that </i>amount of time?. Because, to me, it seems less believable for a conspiration to be hidden for hundreds of years than for a few</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, I don't think it's like they met each other every day or that anyone was particularly looking for corruption within the ranks of the Tremere. And then if you began to suspect that something was wrong, you'd need to figure out exactly what was wrong and then you'd need proof. Unless I'm mistaken, I think they weren't really bothering any of the other mages at this time, so there was little incentive to mess with them. Weren't the actual U.S. Mafia and the extent of their capabilities virtually unknown to the general public except as common criminals until the 1960s, even though they got to the U.S. around 1869?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","name":"Spacecat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12480_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"597"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-11T10:16:02-07:00","id":1076565,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere?p=1076521#post1076521\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> And you have no problem with the Hermetic being fooled <i>that </i>amount of time?. Because, to me, it seems less believable for a conspiration to be hidden for hundreds of years than for a few</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Honestly, I don't. We're talking about Europe in the 1000s and 1100s. Long distance travel and communication is fairly limited, even when we take magic into account. Individual Houses are very insular, distrustful of one another, and secretive.<br /> <br /> Mistridge isn't going to happen until 1210. There aren't a lot of outside threats. So the Houses are their own worst enemy. Most Chantries probably didn't have regular visits. And if someone is scrying at an adversary and encounters an impenetrable field, they're less likely to think &quot;Tremere is turning into a Vampire&quot; than they are &quot;Tremere is plotting something, maybe against us!&quot; and shore up their own defenses instead.<br /> <br /> And then there are a few other factors:<br /> <br /> The Tremere were really good at directing cultural prejudice to easy victims, like Diedne in the Schism War.<br /> <br /> The scholarly nature of Hermetic Magick would have made it relatively unremarkable for the Tremere Council to spend a decade or two holed up in Ceoris &quot;studying&quot;. <br /> <br /> It's not really clear how widespread knowledge of vampirism was among Mages of the time period. It's very possible that should an outside Mage see a Tremere Vampire, they might notice a lot of very strange characteristics, but not the cause. Heck, if the vampire is less pretentious, she can even give an explanation: &quot;We were working on a new immortality potion, but it seems to have backfired. Hopefully this wears off soon because it's really inconvenient!&quot; Actually, Hermetic pride can work in her favor under those circumstances. What Hermetic Mage would want to admit to something that embarrassing if it isn't true?<br /> <br /> Per the source books, the House conversion was fairly slow, which means that when Tremere needed to send a messenger or ambassador, he'd likely have a number of blood bound ghouls who could serve that purpose too and avoid detection. Any weird magical observations can probably be explained away in a similar manner as above.<br /> <br /> It's not foolproof obviously, and per the wiki article (a dubious source, I understand, but sadly I don't have my books in front of me), there were some people who suspected them of diabolism in the 1100s, but they weren't charged. Presumably there wasn't enough proof. And Tremere's ties to Tytalus and Flambeau were probably an advantage too, since those Houses were the most aggressive during the Schism War. We're less likely to see obvious fault in our friends/allies than we are in the people that we dislike.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","name":"kalinara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2430_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"582"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-11T11:15:14-07:00","id":1076592,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kalinara</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere?p=1076471#post1076471\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As mentioned above, it took more than a century for the other Hermetics to realize what's going on. And on the vampire side, the Tremere targets were primarily Tzimisce and Gangrel. The former tend toward isolation and privacy, while the latter tend to be nomadic, so in both cases, it's very possible that individual members could disappear for a while before others in the clans notice and trace their movements enough to detect a pattern. That should buy quite a bit of time to shore up their strength.</div> </div> </div> </div>This, especially.<br /> <br /> It must be remembered that for Kindred, the Tremere are an outside context problem. Cainites and mortal magicians have kept their distance since time in memorial, because both know just enough about each other to be wary. The Tzimisce in particular never gave House Tremere any mind; they competed for few if any resources in the lands they inhabited, and the Hermetics rarely interacted with the spirits of the land. (Hermetics as a whole are more interested in the spirits of the High Umbra).<br /> <br /> For the first few years after the Tremere inner council become vampires, the Tzimisce hardly notice, much less care. The missing elder would more easily be chalked up to reclusive behavior, or having gone into Torpor. Once the death is confirmed, any Tzimisce investigating (which is likely only to be one or two lords at first) would more readily assume another Kindred committed the deed. Those weird wizards doing it would be inconceivable. It's only when the Tzimisce realize there are new vampires running around the lands surrounding Ceoris that the idea would even enter the Tzimice's minds. It would take far longer for the few Tzimisce who know about it to spread that information to the rest of the Clan, AND make them see the gravity of the situation. Why should a Voivoide hundreds of miles away care about magi-turned-vampires? Sure, it's presumptuous and insulting, but there's other work to be done.<br /> <br /> From there, the Bystander Effect kicks in <i>hard</i>. Even Tzimisce lords who care about the Tremere are more willing to let one of their peers take cracks at the fledgling Clan. And if all of them think the same thing, that means they are all paralyzed by inaction. Once someone finally does take action (probably ones who knew the fallen Tzimisce elder personally, and had what passes for fondness among the Clan for them), they wouldn't necessarily commit all their forces to the effort. The Tremere are small and weak - hardly any of their number are vampires, and barely vampires at that. The Tzimisce's rivals, meanwhile, are strong, and ever watchful for opportunities. Those Tzimisce who choose to engage must keep some of their forces and resources in reserve, lest they be blindsided by a peer. Moreover, no one trusts another lord enough to readily work with them, as the army of one could decide to stab the other in the back. Together, the Tzimisce could easily crush this bloodline of usurpers. But Clan Tzimisce is not united. Clan Tremere IS.<br /> <br /> Same goes for the Gangrel, who are even less united in purpose, and have fewer resources to commit to the war effort. They wouldn't even get involved at all, until Gargoyles start getting made (and only after a period of time where the Gangrel figure out where their buddies went).<br /> <br /> All of this adds up to one important fact: the enemies of the Tremere lose the initiative. If they'd struck early and en masse, the Tremere could have done little. But by delaying, it gave the Warlocks the time they needed to develop Thaumaturgy, and all the tricks that power would bring. Bought them time to get their vampiric act together, so the mortal parts of the House wouldn't see what their masters had become. Bought them time to create Gargoyles, and other monsters, and wards against ghouls and spirits and vampires. Bought them time to get help from the rest of the Order of Hermes. Bought them time to trade favors with the Ventrue, who already had a rivalry with the Tzimisce, and had need for magical assistance. Bought them time to locate a sleeping Antedeluvian, and devour them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-11T11:56:52-07:00","id":1076607,"title":"","content":"I have not been able to find a definitive date for when the early Tremere vampires first developed Thaumaturgy. So I started looking for evidence of magical workings of the vampires in the books. The earliest examples are from House of Tremere. In the section &quot;Failed Gambits: 1024-1026&quot; the narrator (a spirit of a deceased mage bound to Ceoris) says to Etrius <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I told him I had observed the exploratory rituals of Goratrix and Epistatia. They'd sensed my presence and tried several times to drive me off </div> </div> </div>So in even the least case that means both Goratrix and Epistatia must have had Thaumaturgy 1 within 2 years of the ritual embrace. Then under &quot;Crisis Within: 1026-1036&quot; it says <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Etrius found himself warding off exploratory magical attacks against him. </div> </div> </div> more evidence that the Tremere had Thaumaturgy within 4 years as vampires and of levels to begin launching attacks against each other. Unfortunately the types and strength of those magics are not given.<br /> <br /> The earliest quantifiable Thaumaturgy example we have is the creation of the Gargoyles both House of Tremere and DAV20 both say that the first Gargoyles appeared in 1121. Since the Gargoyle creation ritual &quot;At Our Command It Breathes&quot; is a Level 5 ritual then Goratrix and Virstania must both have had Thaumaturgy 5. This means Thaumaturgy had been mastered within a century (1022 - 1121 being 99 years). Additionally Virstania is stated as having been born in 1060, a full Mage in 1080 and embraced in 1111 in House of Tremere meaning she reached Thaumaturgy 5 within a decade of embrace.<br /> <br /> The statted vampires in House of Tremere also give some insight into the power the Tremere gained in a short time. Take Mendacamina (sired by Etrius) who was a mortal in 945 and was embraced by Etrius in 1023. In 1197 (174 years after embrace) she has Auspex 3, Dominate 3, and Thaumaturgy 6 with the following Paths<ul><li>Creo Ignem 3, Path of Warding 2, Prey on the Soul's Fear 5, Rego Motus 3, Rego Tempestas 2, Rego Vitae 5</li> </ul><br /> XP wise for Disciplines (we'll assume the 4 'starting dots' went to Thaumaturgy) that means<ul><li>Thaumaturgy 4-5 (20xp), 5-6 (25xp) - 45xp for Thaumaturgy Discipline</li> <li>Secondary Paths (we shall assume Rego Vitae is primary and Prey on the Soul's Fear 5th dot is from Thaum 6)<ul><li>Creo Ignem 1 (7xp), 1-2 (4xp), 2-3 (8xp) - 19xp for Creo Ignem</li> <li>Path of Warding 1 (7xp), 1-2 (4xp) - 11xp for Creo Ignem</li> <li>Prey on Soul's Fear 1 (7xp), 1-2 (4xp), 2-3 (8xp), 3-4 (12xp), 4-5 (Thaum 6 dot) - 31xp for Prey on Soul's Fear</li> <li>Rego Motus 1 (7xp), 1-2 (4xp), 2-3 (8xp) - 19xp for Rego Motus</li> <li>Rego Tempestas 1 (7xp), 1-2 (4xp) - 11xp for Rego Tempestas</li> <li>Rego Vitae 1-5 Thaumaturgy dots</li> <li>136xp in Thaumaturgy</li> </ul></li> <li>Auspex 1 (10xp), 1-2 (5xp), 2-3 (10xp) - 25xp for Auspex Discipline</li> <li>Dominate 1 (10xp), 1-2 (5xp), 2-3 (10xp) - 25xp for Dominate Discipline</li> <li>186 XP total in Disciplines</li> </ul><br /> Another example Therimna (sired by Goratrix) who was a 12 yr old mortal sometime between 850-918 (since it states a noble of Baldwin II of Flanders court purchased her virginity and the WW Wiki puts it at 882) and embraced 1023. In 1197 her Disciplines were Auspex 5, Dominate 5, Serpentis 1, Thaumaturgy 6 with the following Paths<ul><li>Perdo Magica 4, Rego Elementum 2, Rego Magica 4, Rego Vitae 5, Thaumaturgical Alchemy 5 (which HoT also notes is her own creation)</li> </ul><br /> XP math time<ul><li>Thaumaturgy 4-5 (20xp), 5-6 (25xp) - 45xp for Thaumaturgy Discipline</li> <li>Secondary Paths (Assume Rego Vitae primary and Alchemy 5th dot from Thaum 6)<ul><li>Perdo Magica 1 (7xp), 1-2 (4xp), 2-3 (8xp), 3-4 (12xp) - 31xp for Perdo Magica</li> <li>Rego Elementum 1 (7xp), 1-2 (4xp) - 11xp for Rego Elementum</li> <li>Rego Magica 1 (7xp), 1-2 (4xp), 2-3 (8xp), 3-4 (12xp) - 31xp for Rego Magica</li> <li>Rego Vitae 1-5 (Thaum dots)</li> <li>Thaumaturgical Alchemy (7xp), 1-2 (4xp), 2-3 (8xp), 3-4 (12xp), 4-5 (Thaum 6 dot) - 31xp for Thaumaturgical Alchemy</li> <li>149xp in Thaumaturgy</li> </ul></li> <li>Auspex 1 (10xp), 1-2 (5xp), 2-3 (10xp), 3-4 (15xp), 4-5 (20xp) - 60xp for Auspex Discipline</li> <li>Dominate 1 (10xp), 1-2 (5xp), 2-3 (10xp), 3-4 (15xp), 4-5 (20xp) - 60xp for Dominate Discipline</li> <li>Serpentis 1 (10xp) - 10xp for Serpentis Discipline</li> <li>279 XP total in Disciplines</li> </ul><br /> For perspective those are vampires that technically are only Ancillae (less than 200 years as a vampire). In 174 years one of those vampires successfully mastered 3 Disciplines and an elder level dot in one of them and she is less powerful than Tremere, Etrius, and Goratrix.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2008-cynic01","name":"Cynic01","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"437"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-11T20:30:59-07:00","id":1076777,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2008-cynic01\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2008\" data-vbnamecard=\"2008\">Cynic01</a> <br /> <br /> You are mistaken in your assumption I think, to say that a spirit says it witnessed Etrius warding off magical attacks is proof of the development of thaumaturgy is a bridge too far.<br /> <br /> First off, the spirit's words may be misinforming, intentionally or unintentionally.<br /> <br /> Secondly, simply because Etrius, who is a vampire at this point, is creating wards does NOT mean he has developed thaumaturgy, it could be that Etrius has is in the process of doing so and that his efforts may or may not have been effective.<br /> <br /> It is my opinion that the Tremere went back to using the pre awakening hedge magic known as Goetia and Thuergia that they had most likely learned before their awakening. It had become standard practice at that time to teach potential Mages such hedge magic as part of their ore awakening apprenticeship.<br /> <br /> I feel that thaumaturgy is essentially an aberration or permutation of Thuergia and Goetia and that the Tremere might have rediscovered some of this hedge magic rather quickly but that the actual development of reliable thaumaturgy would have taken some time","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3148-zennis","name":"Zennis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3148_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"414"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-11T22:48:36-07:00","id":1076816,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3148-zennis\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar3148_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"3148\" data-vbnamecard=\"3148\">Zennis</a><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Zennis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere?p=1076777#post1076777\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2008-cynic01\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2008\" data-vbnamecard=\"2008\">Cynic01</a><br /> <br /> You are mistaken in your assumption I think, to say that a spirit says it witnessed Etrius warding off magical attacks is proof of the development of thaumaturgy is a bridge too far.<br /> <br /> First off, the spirit's words may be misinforming, intentionally or unintentionally.<br /> <br /> Secondly, simply because Etrius, who is a vampire at this point, is creating wards does NOT mean he has developed thaumaturgy, it could be that Etrius has is in the process of doing so and that his efforts may or may not have been effective.<br /> <br /> It is my opinion that the Tremere went back to using the pre awakening hedge magic known as Goetia and Thuergia that they had most likely learned before their awakening. It had become standard practice at that time to teach potential Mages such hedge magic as part of their ore awakening apprenticeship.<br /> <br /> I feel that thaumaturgy is essentially an aberration or permutation of Thuergia and Goetia and that the Tremere might have rediscovered some of this hedge magic rather quickly but that the actual development of reliable thaumaturgy would have taken some time</div> </div> </div> </div>Given the nature of the dialogue it seems unlikely the spirit is misinforming, but that is not necessarily impossible.<br /> <br /> At no point in the various writings does it say they returned to hedge magick. In some way Thaumaturgy would be an aberration of hedge magics as much as it is an aberration of true magicks since all the early Thaumaturgy was adapted from Hermetic mystical studies. Some examples of what I mean:<br /> <br /> House of Tremere &quot;Thaumaturgic Developments&quot; <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In the two centuries or so since Tremere and his fellows became vampires, much of their efforts have been dedicated to finding ways to preserve and recover their abilities at magical arts. The new blood magic they call Thaumaturgy is the product of that research, which is still on going. In the overall scheme of things, Tremere and the other thaumaturges are still busy adapting hermetic arts and rituals to its demands (using Rego Magica detaled below). Relatively little innovation has taken place, with the notable exception of the blood-centered Rego Vitae path. In a few decades, House Tremere breaks once and for all with the mortal Order of Hermes. These marks the end of the &quot;conversion&quot; phase of thaumaturgical development, both because Tremere and Etrius have exhausted the most viable arts and rituals and because they are banned from the great hermetic libraries. From the middle of the 13th century onward, Thaumaturgy takes its own course separate from that of hermetic lore. </div> </div> </div>So why would so much effort be put into developing such paths if they had access to a full suite of hedge paths? If they had such they would have likely used those and instead focused on developing new Thaumaturgical paths rather than spending time adapting what they could already do.<br /> <br /> Blood Magic Secrets of Thaumaturgy pg 32 <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The founders gained nearly limitless time with which to continue their research, but, ironically, their pursuit of magic led them to lose it. By sacrificing their mortality, they also inadvertently gave up the arcane abilities they so coveted </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Although the grayfaces are closed-mouthed about it, a few years ago I stumbled across an old account of the Tremere's early nights as vampires. The founders Embraced many members of their former House and all, without exception, lost the ability to practice mortal magics </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Warlocks, forced by the loss of their original skills, knew they must quickly develop an appreciable edge or be expunged. The former magi adapted an ancient form of mysticism from their mortal grimoires to their new existence </div> </div> </div>Again more evidence that whatever magic they held from their mortal days they could not access it now that they were vampires.<br /> <br /> Guide to the Low Clans pg 59 <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> They had lost their old magic and must find a new kind. </div> </div> </div>pg 60 <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> while the vampires relearned the Art in a new form </div> </div> </div>pg 186 <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> When the Tremere embraced undeath, much of their magical power faded. The quicker arts of the Pillars flowed readily into sorcerous paths, but lengthier rotes did not convert so easily. </div> </div> </div>None of those statements imply any use of mortal magic, instead making the point that any mortal magics needed to be adapted and relearned under Thaumaturgy.<br /> <br /> Libellus Sanguinus 2 pg 48 <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That moment of fragmentation and the slow, agonizing feeling of dissipation that ensued signified the incorporation of magic into your very essence even as it heralded the death cry or your wizardly power. From a potential worker of magick, you became, instead, a magical creature <i>in essentia </i>no longer able to command the forces or harness the elemental mysteries of the world. </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As individuals and as a clan, we strive to master the knowledge of our newfound methods of magic, which we call Thaumaturgy. Already, we have learned to duplicate much of what we lost in becoming immortal. </div> </div> </div>Both make it clear that what magic they possessed before was lost when they became vampires and they had to develop a new way to do the same effects they had done before.<br /> <br /> Clanbook Tremere Revised pg 13 <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Tremere unearthed ways to apply old magical theories to the new blood. From these roots he developed Thaumaturgy, the means to shape blood and other elements by manipulating the very Curse that animated Kindred. Knowledge of Thaumaturgy spread througout the clan, a replacement for the mystic arts the members of the house had left behind once they became undead. </div> </div> </div>Again the material presents the case that the Tremere lost whatever magic they had and quickly developed a new way to duplicate the effects.<br /> <br /> DAV20 pg 62 <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> the fledgling clan worked rapidly to develop new spells and rituals to protect themselves. The speed with which they created their new Discipline, Thaumaturgy, shocked other vampires. </div> </div> </div>This implies that the Tremere's work in adapting their mortal magics into Thaumaturgy happened very quickly and early in the clan's history.<br /> <br /> From the above as well as the previously mentioned material in my other post we can determine two things. First that a mage embraced by a vampire loses the mortal magics they held though to be fair most of the material implies awakened magic. However in the same way it does not mention that they continue to use other magics they had in life. Second Thaumaturgy was developed to replace the magic they lost. It doesn't say that the Tremere used magic from their mortal days to buy time while they developed Thaumaturgy, nor does it say they developed some aberrant form of magic which they later discarded for Thaumaturgy. All of it says roughly the same thing; that the Tremere lost their mortal magic and raced to adapt and develop a new power which could replicate what they could accomplish previously.<br /> <br /> Additionally Thaumaturgy's early paths and rituals (those listed in DAV materials) were directly adapted from the various magics they had studied and used in their mortal life as mages. As stated in my previous post Gargoyles were developed in 1121 thus Thaumaturgy 5 must have been achieved by that point. Considering that Thaumaturgy 5 had to have been reached by 1121 and the number of Paths (which have 5 levels listed) and Rituals available in the Dark Ages (1197 in the original Vampire: Dark Ages) I can only assume that the fundamentals of Thaumaturgy must have developed fairly quickly which is reinforced by the bit in Guide to the Low Clans about the quicker Pillars flowing easily into sorcerous paths and the DAV20 mention of the speed of Thaumaturgy's development shocking other vampires. The converting of magical power to XP seems to help explain how the Tremere developed Thaumaturgy so quickly. While they lost the magic they held, they still possessed all the knowledge and framework around that magic. Once they started applying it to vampiric vitae they rapidly found they could create similar effects to what they used to have, which would lead them to continue to convert the other powers they once had hence Thaumaturgy 5 in under a century and a multitude of full paths in well under two centuries.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2008,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2008-cynic01","user":"Cynic01","date":". 04-12-2017, 01:51 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2008-cynic01","name":"Cynic01","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"437"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-12T01:47:07-07:00","id":1076857,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2008-cynic01\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2008\" data-vbnamecard=\"2008\">Cynic01</a> you have made a very good series of points but I think it's possible that you're misinterpreting the material.<br /> <br /> First off, vampires can practice hedge magic just like mortals can. It's entirely possible to do so. I think the references to the vampire loosing their magic is in explicit reference to AWAKENED magic but that's my opinion. I see no reason why a sorcerer who was embraced would loose his or her mastery over linear magic. <br /> <br /> Secondly: Going back to hedge magic after centuries wouldn't be easy, it'd be like relearning or learning ANEW something that had been abandoned by centuries ago. Especially if the Tremere want to find a way to fuel it with vitae, not willpower, which is what powers hedge magic.<br /> <br /> Thirdly: it seems these statements you have quoted do not make specific reference to sleeper/hedge magic or awakened magick, nor do they reference the loss of the avatar or the ensuing effects.<br /> <br /> It is possibly you are correct however and I salute your thoroughness in citing the source material, that was impressive.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3148,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3148-zennis","user":"Zennis","date":". 04-12-2017, 04:52 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3148-zennis","name":"Zennis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3148_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"414"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-12T06:23:55-07:00","id":1076928,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Zennis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere?p=1076857#post1076857\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2008-cynic01\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2008\" data-vbnamecard=\"2008\">Cynic01</a> you have made a very good series of points but I think it's possible that you're misinterpreting the material.<br /> <br /> First off, vampires can practice hedge magic just like mortals can. It's entirely possible to do so. .</div> </div> </div> </div>Where does it say this, by the way? (In Sorcerer, it seems to have been left &quot;entirely up to you as the Storyteller&quot; if any supernaturals can practice Sorcery)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12480,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","user":"Spacecat","date":". 04-12-2017, 09:43 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","name":"Spacecat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12480_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"597"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-12T06:31:03-07:00","id":1076931,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Spacecat</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere?p=1076928#post1076928\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Where does it say this, by the way?</div> </div> </div> </div>Nowhere. There is a 5 point merit in Wolves of the Sea which allows a vampire to imitate sorcery in a limited way through runecraft. You still need to buy sorcery paths as normal and unlike the sorcerer you have to carve runes for each casting, which takes time. But there is no general permission for vampires to learn sorcery.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12892-kammerer","name":"Kammerer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12892_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1191"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-12T09:15:29-07:00","id":1077013,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Zennis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere?p=1076857#post1076857\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> First off, vampires can practice hedge magic just like mortals can. It's entirely possible to do so.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's not actually true. There are a couple of Merits out there that allow for limited replication of hedge magic abilities, but the only vampires who can freely practice hedge magic are Dhampirs (who are also mentioned to have a particular affinity for it).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2507-caitiff-primogen","name":"Caitiff Primogen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2507_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1351"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-13T02:43:09-07:00","id":1077591,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Spacecat</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere?p=1076928#post1076928\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Where does it say this, by the way? (In Sorcerer, it seems to have been left &amp;quot;entirely up to you as the Storyteller&amp;quot; if any supernaturals can practice Sorcery)</div> </div> </div> </div>Wolves of the sea<br /> <br /> It's not even an expensive merit!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3148-zennis","name":"Zennis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3148_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"414"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-13T02:46:19-07:00","id":1077594,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Zennis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere?p=1077591#post1077591\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Wolves of the sea<br /> <br /> It's not even an expensive merit!</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, but it's an obscure merit from a different time period with a very focused form of magic.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13009-burritomage","name":"BurritoMage","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13009_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"585"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-13T03:08:10-07:00","id":1077606,"title":"","content":"It's not a merit I'd expect to be widespread amongst the Tremere. No, I don't buy that the Tremere could work Hedge Magick.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-13T03:17:20-07:00","id":1077610,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere?p=1077606#post1077606\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's not a merit I'd expect to be widespread amongst the Tremere. No, I don't buy that the Tremere could work Hedge Magick.</div> </div> </div> </div>Exactly, some retainers yes because that how the OoH trains it's apprentices until they awaken. The whole hermetic paradigm is based off of the will of the wielder, applying that to the blood is the basis of thaumaturgy.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13009-burritomage","name":"BurritoMage","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13009_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"585"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-13T07:33:12-07:00","id":1077719,"title":"","content":"They did bring that merit back for Bastet and Kitsune in CB20 (after a fashion).<br /> <br /> Now, I still don't see a huge point in it, as it's rarely got anything a Vampire with Thaumurtagy can't already do better and with less XP.<br /> <br /> Now Tremere acolytes and ghouls on the other hand...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5818-eviltyger","name":"EvilTyger","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5818_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"314"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-14T15:06:30-07:00","id":1078561,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere?p=1077606#post1077606\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's not a merit I'd expect to be widespread amongst the Tremere. No, I don't buy that the Tremere could work Hedge Magick.</div> </div> </div> </div>I wasn't suggesting they could, I was talking about vampires having the ability to work hedge magic, a proposition which was being discussed as potentially impossible.<br /> <br /> Thus, I made reference to the fact that it is possible.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3148-zennis","name":"Zennis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3148_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"414"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}